text:
maria: hey team — hitting a weird DX snag in the sample app for the js/ts SDK. when consuming the package in an ESM-only edge project (vercel edge), our overloads + generics basically disappear at compile-time and errors show up at runtime. anyone seen this?

aj: ugh, yep. bundlers + dual-publish can be brutal. what does your package.json + tsconfig look like?

maria: package.json uses "exports" with ./dist/esm and ./dist/cjs, and we ship types in dist/types. tsconfig has "module": "ESNext", "target": "ES2020". in the edge sample, typescript is resolving the wrong overload and esbuild removes the helper. small repro: 

```ts
// sdk-client.ts (simplified)
export function callApi(body: string): string;
export function callApi<T>(body: T): T;
export function callApi(body: any) {
  return JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(body));
}

// usage in edge.ts
import { callApi } from 'redwood-sdk';
const x = callApi({ foo: 1 }); // inferred as any in the edge bundle, loses generic
```

maria: compile-time OK locally, but in the edge sample user reported runtime type surprises.

dmitri: two things to check: 1) ensure you ship a declaration file that matches your ESM entry (types field or per export types using "exports" -> { "types": ... }), and 2) tsconfig paths / moduleResolution differences between consumers. many bundlers use node16 resolution which can pick cjs entry unexpectedly.

sam: also consider avoiding overloads for public surface if possible. Overloads are erased at runtime and bundlers sometimes collapse signatures. A single generic signature is more robust: 

```ts
export function callApi<T = any>(body: T): T {
  return JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(body)) as T;
}
```

lina: +1. For edge (esm-only) we found making the package ESM-first and adding a small shim for CJS consumers reduces surprises. Also add a tiny README note in the sample that explains consumers should set "moduleResolution" to "bundler" or "node16" depending on their toolchain.

maria: curious about unknown vs any — we use any in some internal helpers and that might leak. Would switching to unknown help with inference?

dmitri: unknown is safer at boundaries — it forces callers to assert types. But it can also make generics more verbose. For SDK surface I prefer explicit generics with defaults and using unknown in internal untyped spots. Eg: 

```ts
export function parseResponse<T = unknown>(raw: unknown): T {
  // runtime checks here
  return raw as T;
}
```

aj: oh and package.json trick that fixed us: add "typesVersions" to help older tscs pick the right entry, and add this exports shape: 

```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/esm/index.js",
    "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js",
    "types": "./dist/types/index.d.ts"
  }
}
```

sam: for the sample repo, maybe include a tiny tsconfig.override.json that consumers can drop in when running the sample to simulate an edge build. Saves a lot of "it works locally" tickets.

maria: great tips — anyone want to pair for 20m to update the sample and add a note in the DX guide? I can open a PR.

lina: i can pair after lunch. also add a test in the sample that runs esbuild with the exact flags we expect.

ci-bot: build/test for branch maria/edge-typing-fix started — job 3492 queued. :gear:

aj: thx. one more: document the recommendation to prefer a single generic over overloads for public SDK functions and show the "unknown boundary + internal any shim" pattern. saves a ton of time.

maria: will do. I'll draft a tiny section and the sample changes. thanks everyone :rocket:
